At Mon, 10 Oct 2005 19:19:38 +0530,
<pradeep.gane@wipro.com> wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I am using the mmap feature of ALSA framework (without PCI DMA). I am
> expecting an interrupt after data of scheduled size is transferred by
> the hardware. I am supposed to call the snd_pcm_period_elapsed() API in
> the interrupt handler. 
> 
> My questions are...
> 
> 1 - Is the snd_pcm_period_elapsed call sufficient for my driver? 

Usually yes.  snd_pcm_period_elapsed() does the most of jobs in the
middle layer.


> 2 - How are error conditions like under-run and over-run supposed to be
> passed to the higher layers? Should I call snd_pcm_period_elapsed in
> this case as well?

If you know any XRUN condition prior to snd_pcm_period_elapsed(), then
you can call snd_pcm_stop(substream, SNDRV_PCM_STATE_XRUN).  This will
stop the stream and set its state to XRUN.  See atiixp.c as an
example.

snd_pcm_period_elapsed() checks the return value of pointer callback
and calculates the update position.  This is a normal check for
XRUNs. 
In addition, the driver can tell the middle layer the XRUN condition
explicitly via pointer callback by returning SNDRV_PCM_POS_XRUN, too.


